<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirty Comrades</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thirty Comrades were young Burmese nationalists, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>, who received secret military training from the Japanese on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a> in 1941. In December 1941 they founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> with the Japanese invasion of 1942. Several became leading figures of independent Burma, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a>, who seized power in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades.jpg" alt="Thirty Comrades" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burma_Independence_Army.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
